title: Network and special filesystems
tags: Platform
kind: reference
aliases: nfs smb sshfs fuse
summary: The store refuses a network filesystem, the tracked files do not; FUSE, removable and read-only volumes each have their own rules.

!! Where `.metafolder/` may live

The store is memory-mapped and locked with `flock` ([[Storage]], [[The repository lock]]),
and neither is safe over a network: a `.metafolder/` on NFS, SMB/CIFS, SSHFS, 9p or a
cluster filesystem is ''refused'' at `init` and at load ([[Storage safety]] has the
list). The test is a list of known filesystem types, read from `/proc/self/mountinfo`:
FUSE counts only for the remote kinds it names (`fuse.sshfs`, `fuse.rclone`, `fuse.s3fs`…),
so a local drive through `fuse.ntfs-3g` is accepted — and so is a remote FUSE filesystem
missing from the list. Where the mount table cannot be read (no `/proc`), nothing is
refused.

!! Where the files may live

Anywhere readable. For files on a share, keep `.metafolder/` on a local disk, as an
external one:

```
mf repo init /mnt/nas/photos --metafolder ~/metafolder/photos
```

What a remote root costs:

* ''Watching'' may see nothing: inotify reports only the changes made through this machine's mount, never those made by the server or another client. A `mf reconcile` catches up ([[Reconcile]]).
* ''Reconcile and fingerprints'' read over the network: a first reconcile of a large share is slow.
* ''Case sensitivity'' is the server's, and is probed like any other ([[Case sensitivity]]).

!! Removable volumes

A volume mounted inside a tracked tree is a ''mount point'': while it is unplugged, its
subtree is frozen rather than read as deleted ([[Mount points]]). A whole repository on a
removable drive moves with it ([[Moving a repository]]).

!! Read-only volumes

The files may be read-only — reconcile and the watcher only read them. `.metafolder/`
may not: the store and its lock file are opened for writing at every load, so a repository whose `.metafolder/` is
on read-only media must use an external one.
